t a health check — e.g. a\ngenerated tool that records a decision or returns a namespace's status map, with the DuckBrain\nnamespace named. If the scope chose (c), prove the wrapper drives the real CLI and that a refusal\n(unknown option, unknown namespace) surfaces as a tool error rather than a silent success.\n\n             DELIVERABLE — implementation plus a LIVE proof:\n             1. land the spec + parity check the spec row froze\n             2. build muster from source and demonstrate its OWN binaries against this service:\n                - `openapi-mcp --spec <ours>` → the typed tools are listed and at least one READ tool\n                  executes against the live service and returns real data\n                - `openapi-cli <generated-verb>` → a typed command executes and prints a real response\n                - the generated CLI must be driven through the documented invocation, not a bespoke\n                  shell script standing in for it\n             3. paste the raw output of both as this row's evidence, with the exact commands\n             4. name any operation muster generates that an agent should NOT be given, and how it is\n                withheld (per the scope row's boundary call)\n\n             ACCEPTANCE: a verifier who has never seen this repo can reproduce the two muster\n             binaries working against the live service from the row's own text. A spec that parses\n             but whose generated tools fail against the service is a FAIL, not a partial.\n\n             NOTE: muster is PRIVATE (github.com/wojons/muster) — the build row needs GOPRIVATE +\n             GitHub auth; record the working incantation in the row.", "capability_tags": ["+integration", "+protocol", "+interop"], "attempts": 0, "blocked_reason": null, "blocked_since": null, "blocks": [], "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "completed_at": null, "depends_on": ["AUG-066"], "dispatched_at": null, "exit_code": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "perpetual": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "review_notes": null, "worker_status": "pending", "worker_summary": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "0c77918502f28364612c12a9b2f087d83496c2c2b6f03a83280e7d5a8cde9a61"}, "worktree": null, "branch": null, "superseded_by": null}
 {"id": "AUG-068", "title": "[P1] DuckBrain declared-table selects silently cap at 100 rows: status/dump under-count and drop real rows past the page size (no truncation signal, no pagination)", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "DO-7 scale leg, namespace auger-df7-scale: after 60 answers x 3 options, `auger status` reported 'options 100' and `auger dump` rendered D-031 with 2 of its 3 options; the store held 180 correct rows (verified via explicit limit=1000 curl: 100 returned by default, limit=101 -> 101, limit=180 -> 180; no Content-Range header, HTTP 200, zero truncation signal). cmd_status and cmd_dump call select() with NO limit, so any auger project past 100 option/decision rows silently mis-reports. Fix direction: paginate every unbounded select in auger.py (limit+offset loop, or re-fetch with a doubled limit when len==limit — equality is the only signal the API gives); upstream substrate could emit Content-Range. Related: AUG-022 already flags count-based health checks.", "status": "pending", "created_at": "2026-09-24 18:40:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24 18:40:00"}
-{"id": "AUG-069", "title": "[P0] answer's count-based id mint freezes ALL writes past 100 decisions ('D-101 already exists' forever) — uses len(select()), the exact bug next_id() exists to avoid", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "DO-7 scale+concurrency leg: after the 100th decision, every answer — including a single writer after the concurrent leg ended — is refused with \"refused: decision 'D-101' already exists\". cmd_answer mints `did = f\"D-{len(select(ns,'decision', project filter))+1:03d}\"`; that unpaginated select caps at 100 rows, so the mint sticks at D-101 while the real next id is D-102+. next_id() (auger.py:519) already does the right thing (order=id.desc&limit=1, page-safe) and its docstring names the count-based approach as 'the classic bug this deliberately does not have' — cmd_answer just doesn't use it. Permanent write lockout for any project past 100 decisions; only recovery is explicit --id. Fix: mint the default id via next_id() and paginate any count kept for reporting.", "status": "pending", "created_at": "2026-09-24 18:40:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24 18:40:00"}
